      "t": "Он очень жестокий.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Что же мне делать?",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let us examine the conversation line by line.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Что с телефонами?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What's with your phones?' Russian leaves out 'am', 'is', or 'are' in the present tense, linking 'what' straight to 'telephone'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я не могу дозвониться.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I can't get through.' Notice the word order: 'I' comes first, and 'to get through' finishes the sentence.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Мой телефон украли.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'My phone was stolen.' Here 'phone' sets up the idea, while 'stole' comes at the end for emphasis. Nastya answers straight away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Мне звонила мама.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Mom called me.' Nastya puts 'to me' up front so the listener immediately catches the tone, followed by 'called'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Папа познакомился с Костей.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Dad met Kostya.' Listen for the word meaning 'Dad' right alongside 'to get acquainted' in this line.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Но он думает, что это ты.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'But he thinks it's you.' Nastya uses the casual singular 'you' with 'he', as close colleagues and friends do. Then Max replies.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я не понял.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I don't get it.' The key word here means 'I', which Max combines with 'understood?'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Он думает, что ты — Костя.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'He thinks that you are Kostya.' The first content word means 'he', and 'Kostya' rounds out the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Зачем он приходил?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Why did he come?' A compact expression built around 'what for' and 'used to come'. And Kostya jumps in.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Он угрожал мне.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'He threatened me.' A compact expression built around 'he' and 'to me'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Скажи ему правду.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Tell him the truth.' Max starts with 'say' and pairs it with 'him' to make the point clear.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Я не могу.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I can't.' The little negative word sits right in front of the verb for 'can', just like 'not' in English.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Он очень жестокий.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'He is very cruel.' Notice the word order: 'he' comes first, and 'cruel' finishes the sentence.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Что же мне делать?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What should I do?' Here 'what' sets up the idea, while 'do' comes at the end for emphasis. Now let us listen to the conversation once again.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
